{{ElectionsMI}}
The '''2018 United States House of Representatives elections in Michigan''' will be held on November 6, 2018, to elect the 14 [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. Representatives]] from the [[U.S. state|state]] of [[Michigan]], one from each of the state's 14 [[Michigan's congressional districts|congressional districts]]. The elections will coincide with the [[Michigan gubernatorial election, 2018|gubernatorial election]], as well as [[United States House of Representatives elections, 2018|other elections]] to the House of Representatives, [[United States Senate elections, 2018|elections]] to the [[United States Senate]] in 33 other states and various [[United States elections, 2018#State elections|state]] and [[United States elections, 2018#Local elections|local elections]]. The filing deadline for candidates filing for the August 7 primary was April 24, 2018.<ref>http://www.michigan.gov/documents/sos/2018_Dates_600221_7.pdf</ref> Unless otherwise indicated, the [[Cook Political Report]] has rated the congressional races as safe for the party of the incumbent.

{{Toclimit|limit=2}}

==District 1==
{{see also|Michigan's 1st congressional district}}
The 1st district consists of the entire [[Upper Peninsula of Michigan|Upper Peninsula]] of [[Michigan]] and the [[Northern Michigan|northern part]] of the [[Lower Peninsula of Michigan|Lower Peninsula]] including [[Alpena, MI|Alpena]], [[Marquette, MI|Marquette]], and [[Traverse City, MI|Traverse City]]. This district has a PVI of R+9. The district, which makes up about 44% of the land area of the state of Michigan, is the [[List of United States congressional districts by area|second-largest congressional district]] east of the [[Mississippi River]] by land area. The incumbent is Republican [[Jack Bergman]], who has represented the district since 2017. He was elected to replace retiring Representative [[Dan Benishek]] with 55% of the vote in 2016. The Cook Political Report has rated this race 'likely Republican.'<ref name="cookpolitical.com">{{cite web|url=https://www.cookpolitical.com/ratings/house-race-ratings|title=2018 House Race ratings - The Cook Political Report|website=The Cook Political Report|accessdate=August 29, 2018}}</ref>

===Democratic primary===
====Candidates====
Matt Morgan was the only Democrat to file to run. However, Morgan was removed from the ballot because he used a [[PO box]] address on his nomination petitions instead of his residential address. Instead, Morgan ran a write-in campaign, hoping to qualify for the ballot by winning at least five percent of the total votes cast in the district for the Democratic gubernatorial primary. <ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.freep.com/story/news/politics/2018/06/01/matt-morgan-congress-michigan-ballot/663363002/|title=Only Democrat in Michigan race will have to run as write-in candidate|publisher=|accessdate=August 29, 2018}}</ref>  Over 4,800 votes were cast in [[Marquette County, Michigan|Marquette County]], which would have been enough by itself to qualify Morgan for the ballot.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.record-eagle.com/news/local_news/matt-morgan-declares-write-in-campaign-a-success/article_cd894728-8ad7-5d90-83b3-13993f8e6b58.html|title=Matt Morgan declares write-in campaign a success|first=JORDAN TRAVIS|last=jtravis@record-eagle.com|publisher=|accessdate=August 29, 2018}}</ref>  According to official results, Democrats cast 29,293 write-in votes in the primary for Morgan, more than seven times the 3,781-vote threshold. On August 24, the Board of State Canvassers placed Morgan on the November ballot. <ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlive.com/news/index.ssf/2018/08/democrat_matt_morgan_will_be_o.html|title=Democrat Matt Morgan will be on ballot in 1st Congressional District|publisher=|accessdate=August 29, 2018}}</ref>
;Failed to qualify for primary; ran successful write-in campaign to qualify for general election
* Matthew W. Morgan, retired US Marine Corps lieutenant colonel and Iraq War veteran<ref name="nictusa.com">{{cite web|url=http://miboecfr.nictusa.com/election/candlist/2018PRI_CANDLIST.html|title=2018 Michigan Official Primary Candidate Listing - 08/07/2018|website=miboecfr.nictusa.com|accessdate=August 29, 2018}}</ref>
;Withdrew
* Dwight Brady, professor<ref name="nictusa.com"/>

